WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
Hey, welcome to fascinating frontiers, Episode 133.

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.800
Today is July 16th, 2026.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:09.820
Let's look at what's new across the frontiers

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.120
of space and science.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.620
Astronomers have directly imaged a third giant planet

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:18.680
in the beta-pictoris system by spotting its carbon monoxide

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.800
signature with JWST.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:25.980
JWST captured the new world, designated beta-pick D,

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.780
while observers were studying the atmosphere

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:31.120
of the known planet beta-pick B.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.260
An unexpected blob appeared with the clear spectral signature

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:36.500
of carbon monoxide.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.980
Its motion, location, and alignment with the star's debris disc

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:44.480
confirmed it as a bound planet rather than a background object.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.800
Researchers will now use additional web data

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.180
to refine the object's temperature, atmospheric makeup,

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:52.460
and orbital parameters.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.120
The bright debris disc had previously scattered light

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.880
and hidden such companions from earlier telescopes.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.180
Follow-up observations are already scheduled

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:03.700
to tighten the orbit solution.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:07.280
This marks the third directly-image planet in the system

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.720
and adds a new data point to studies

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.440
of giant planet formation around young stars.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.840
JWST's ability to ignore the bright debris disc

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.680
and isolate the planet's carbon monoxide signature

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.400
made the find possible after earlier telescopes had missed it.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.240
Refined temperature and orbit measurements

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.440
expected from upcoming observations

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.900
will test whether the planet formed in place or migrated.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.160
How many more hidden worlds remain undetected

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.480
in other bright discs?

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:40.040
If the sun were replaced by a typical young star

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.180
still surrounded by its birth disc,

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:45.380
the nearest planet-forming zone would sit inside

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:47.320
a glowing ring of dust and gas,

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.700
no wider than the orbit of Jupiter,

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.300
yet containing enough material to build several earths

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:56.940
inside that ring, micron-sized grains collide and stick,

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.260
growing into planetesimals while the leftover dust

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.820
scatters starlight into a bright haze

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:06.300
that can outshine any embedded world by factors of thousands.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:10.380
Telescopes on the ground must therefore block the star's glare

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.820
with coronagraphs and subtract the disc's speckled light

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:14.620
in software.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.380
Even then, a forming planet only becomes visible

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:21.460
once it clears a gap or heats a local patch of gas

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:23.860
enough to produce a distinct spectral line

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:25.360
such as carbon monoxide.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:29.440
The beta-pictoris system shows this process in action

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.780
because its disc is so luminous that earlier instruments

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.080
could not separate the new planet's signal

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:39.200
until JWST observed at mid-infrared wavelengths

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:40.620
where the dust is fainter.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:44.600
The remaining puzzle is why some discs reveal their planets

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:47.700
quickly while others stay opaque for millions of years.

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:51.160
What sets the clearing timescale that decides

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:54.060
whether we ever get to watch a giant planet grow.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.320
NASA will stream live from the clean room

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:00.160
where the Dragonfly rotor craft is being assembled

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.400
for its mission to Saturn's moon Titan.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:05.820
The event is set for 2 p.m. Eastern time

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:07.720
on the agency's Twitch channel.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.000
Dragonfly will use eight rotors to fly between multiple sites

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.360
on Titan, sampling surface materials

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.480
and measuring atmospheric properties.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:20.500
The preview offers the first public look at the flight vehicle

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.640
in its final assembly configuration.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:26.060
This update shows hardware integration progress

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:27.660
on an outer planet mission.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.260
The Flex fluorescence mapper and Copernicus Sentinel-3C

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:35.680
Ocean and Land satellite have arrived at Europe's

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.680
spaceport ahead of a September launch on Vega-C.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:42.160
Teams will spend the coming weeks performing inspections

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.800
and integrating the spacecraft with their rocket.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.600
Both missions will deliver new Earth observation data

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:49.560
once in orbit.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:52.940
The satellites represent complementary capabilities

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:55.900
with Flex focused on vegetation fluorescence

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:00.080
and Sentinel-3C extending ocean and land monitoring records.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.480
After more than a decade of targeted searches,

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:05.880
astronomers recorded the dimest planet

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:08.040
ever directly imaged from the ground.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:11.320
The object appeared as a faint moving spot

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:13.820
in repeated high contrast observations.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:17.940
Its extreme faintness required long integration times

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:20.660
and advanced adaptive optics techniques

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:23.200
to separate it from the host star's glare.

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:26.560
Further measurements will determine its mass

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:27.840
and orbital distance.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:31.200
Scott's astronomers contributed to the discovery

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.020
of this faint exoplanet in a distant system.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:37.680
A new study shows that current ground-based methods

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.180
cannot yet determine how fast supermassive black holes

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:41.960
are rotating.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:45.620
The authors demonstrate that a future space-based

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:48.100
X-ray observatory could measure the spin

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:51.680
by tracking iron line profiles close to the event horizon.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:54.660
The work highlights the need for stable,

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.920
high-resolution spectroscopy above the atmosphere.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:01.060
Determining spin rates is described as key

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:03.280
to understanding how black holes affect

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:05.520
their immediate vicinity and the galaxies

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:06.500
that surround them.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:10.980
SpaceX plans a suborbital starship super heavy flight

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:14.500
that will deploy the initial Starlink V3 satellites

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:18.220
while testing hardware upgrades aimed at full reusability.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:21.480
Lift off from star-based Texas is targeted

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:25.400
inside a 90-minute window opening at 5.45 pm

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:26.740
central daylight time.

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:29.200
The mission will provide an early look

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.100
at the larger higher capacity Starlink design.

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:36.660
This step advances testing of orbital refueling prerequisites

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:39.440
and rapid reuse cadence on a flight

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:41.980
that also carries operational payloads.

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:44.680
A Falcon 9 will carry 21 satellites

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:46.760
for the Space Development Agency's

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:50.740
Tranch-1 Transporter Layer from Vandenberg Space Force Base.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.660
The satellites form the third operational batch

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:56.940
supporting the proliferated warfighter space architecture.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.340
Launch is scheduled for 1.32 pm Pacific daylight time.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.920
The mission continues the commercial space flight emphasis

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:08.100
on constellation deployment cadence and cost reduction.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:11.500
New models show that galactic spiral arms

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:15.360
and central bars act as efficient pumps that move gas inward,

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.600
fueling the high star formation rates

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:19.500
observed during cosmic noon.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:22.380
The structures create shocks and torques

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:25.040
that concentrate molecular clouds on time scales

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:26.660
of a few hundred million years.

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:30.500
The findings help explain why star formation activity

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:32.880
peaked between two and three billion years

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:34.000
after the Big Bang.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:38.000
Peak rates during that era reached up to 100 times

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:39.220
the present day value.

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:43.580
NASA continues to integrate SLS and Orion components

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:47.120
while running launch day scenarios inside mission control

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:49.820
for the 2027 crude lunar landing.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:53.600
The agency reports steady progress on both the lander

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:55.880
and gateway elements needed for the mission.

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:59.380
These steps address the remaining schedule questions

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:01.760
for returning astronauts to the moon's surface.

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:05.860
This update moves the Artemis and the moon program forward

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:08.320
by demonstrating concrete hardware assembly

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:11.720
and operational rehearsal progress toward the crude landing

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:12.260
schedule.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.700
The rover photographed an unusual honeycomb

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:19.100
like texture on a rock surface inside gale crater.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:21.980
The pattern's origin remains unclear

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:25.500
with possible explanations ranging from mineral precipitation

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:26.680
to wind erosion.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:30.240
Mission scientists are examining additional images

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:33.640
to determine whether the feature is widespread or isolated.

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:37.720
The observation adds to the robotic exploration record

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:40.340
of surface processes in gale crater.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:44.180
The Dale Reed Subscale Flight Research Laboratory

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:46.360
at Armstrong Flight Research Center

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:48.760
flies small, remotely piloted aircraft

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:52.160
to evaluate new aerospace concepts at low cost.

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:56.080
These vehicles allow engineers to mature technologies

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:58.840
and reduce risk before full-scale development.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:03.020
Recent flights have focused on autonomy and novel wing designs.

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:06.120
The approach supports NASA's broader goal

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:08.080
of accelerating flight innovation

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:10.400
through repeated inexpensive testing.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:14.820
A robotic servicing spacecraft is completing in-orbit checks

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:18.780
before raising NASA's Neil Garell's swift observatory

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:19.900
to a higher altitude.

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:23.680
The mission will extend the satellite's operational life

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:25.880
by moving it out of residual atmosphere.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:28.580
Teams report that commissioning activities

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:30.080
are proceeding on schedule.

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:32.720
The altitude rays will reduce drag

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:35.560
and allow continued multi-wave length observations

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:37.200
of transient events.

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:41.120
Rocket Lab completed a full-duration static fire test

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:44.300
of the Archimedes engine that will power the second stage

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:46.740
of its upcoming neutron launch vehicle.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:49.660
The test demonstrated stable combustion

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:52.260
and throttling across the required duration.

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:56.260
Data from the firing will support final design validation

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:57.920
for the medium-lift rocket.

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:01.040
The engine is intended to enable neutron's role

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:03.480
in commercial and government launch markets.

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:05.880
A broken circuit breaker switch

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:08.460
and the felt-tip pen used to activate it

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:12.540
during Apollo 11 sold together for $860,000.

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:15.740
The items illustrate the improvised solution

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:18.780
that allowed the lunar module to lift off from the moon.

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:22.000
The sale underscores continuing public interest

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:23.180
in the mission's hardware.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:25.840
The pen was used to close the circuit

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:27.720
after the original switch failed.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:31.680
Plumes from Ontario fires drifted southeastward,

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:34.220
crossing Quebec and reaching parts of the US,

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:35.680
Midwest and Northeast.

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:38.660
Satellite imagery tracked the smoke's path

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:41.020
and aerosol loading over several days.

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:43.880
The event provides fresh data on how

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:46.880
boreal fire emissions affect regional air quality.

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:50.360
NASA Earth-observing instruments captured the movement

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:52.660
of the smoke across international boundaries.

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:56.040
Next time, keep an eye on the first public views

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:58.800
of Dragonfly in its flight-ready configuration

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:01.680
and the upcoming Vega C launch window.

